===============================================================================
PART 1 : THE TWO KEYWORDS
===============================================================================

`abstract` is used in exactly two places.

ON A METHOD -- declare it, do not define it

        abstract void start();          // signature, semicolon, NO braces

    Not this:

        abstract void start() { }       // ERROR -- abstract methods cannot
                                        // have a body, not even an empty one

ON A CLASS -- mark it as incomplete, so it cannot be instantiated

        abstract class Vehicle { ... }

THE LINK BETWEEN THEM

        A class with even ONE abstract method MUST be declared abstract.
        An abstract class is NOT required to have any abstract methods.

    The arrow only points one way. Remember it as:

        abstract method  ==>  abstract class      (forced)
        abstract class   ==>  abstract method     (NOT forced)


===============================================================================
PART 2 : WHAT AN ABSTRACT CLASS CAN AND CANNOT HAVE
===============================================================================

    CAN HAVE                                CANNOT
    ------------------------------------    ---------------------------------
    abstract methods                        objects of itself (new Vehicle())
    concrete methods (with bodies)          to be final     (abstract + final)
    instance fields (any modifier)          abstract constructors
    static fields and constants             abstract static methods
    a constructor (see PART 3)              abstract private methods
    static methods                          abstract final methods
    final methods
    private methods
    initializer blocks (static + instance)
    a main() method -- and it will run
    to implement an interface WITHOUT
      writing its methods
    to extend another class
    to be extended by another abstract class

That fourth item on the right -- "to implement an interface without writing
its methods" -- is genuinely useful. The unimplemented interface methods
simply stay abstract and the first concrete subclass has to write them.

        interface Payable { double pay(); }

        abstract class Employee implements Payable {
            // no pay() here at all. Legal, because Employee is abstract.
        }

        class Manager extends Employee {
            public double pay() { return 90000; }    // NOW it is required
        }


===============================================================================
PART 3 : CONSTRUCTORS -- THE QUESTION EVERYONE GETS WRONG
===============================================================================

    Q: Can an abstract class have a constructor?
    A: YES.

    Q: But you can never create an object of it, so who calls it?
    A: The SUBCLASS does, through super(...), every single time a subclass
       object is created.

This is not a special rule. It is the constructor chaining you already did
in Constructor/ConstructorChaining. Nothing new is happening.

        abstract class Vehicle {
            private final String model;

            Vehicle(String model) {              // runs on every subclass
                this.model = model;              // object creation
                System.out.println("Vehicle constructor");
            }
        }

        class Car extends Vehicle {
            Car(String model) {
                super(model);                    // this line calls it
                System.out.println("Car constructor");
            }
        }

        new Car("Swift");        ->   Vehicle constructor
                                      Car constructor

WHY IT MATTERS -- this is the reason to pick an abstract class over an
interface. The constructor lets the parent initialise SHARED STATE and
enforce a class invariant for every subclass at once:

        Vehicle(String model) {
            if (model == null || model.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Model cannot be empty");
                model = "UNKNOWN";
            }
            this.model = model;
        }

    Now no subclass, present or future, can exist with an empty model.
    An interface cannot do this. It has no fields and no constructor.

THE ORDER TO REMEMBER

        1. parent's static blocks     (once, when the class loads)
        2. child's static blocks      (once)
        3. parent's instance blocks + field initialisers
        4. parent's constructor body
        5. child's instance blocks + field initialisers
        6. child's constructor body

    You proved this yourself in BlocksInJava/StaticBlockTest.java. Being
    abstract changes nothing about it.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : THE RULES FOR SUBCLASSES
===============================================================================

When class B extends abstract class A, B has exactly two legal choices:

    CHOICE 1 -- implement EVERY abstract method inherited from A.
                B is then concrete and can be instantiated.

    CHOICE 2 -- implement some or none of them, and declare B abstract too.
                The unimplemented methods pass down to B's own subclasses.

        abstract class Shape {
            abstract double area();
            abstract double perimeter();
        }

        abstract class TwoDShape extends Shape {
            double perimeter() { return 0; }    // handles one...
                                                // ...leaves area() abstract
        }                                       // so it MUST stay abstract

        class Square extends TwoDShape {
            double area() { return 4; }         // now the last one is done
        }                                       // Square can be instantiated

There is no third option. "Forgetting" is choice 2 without saying so, and
the compiler rejects it with:

        Bike is not abstract and does not override abstract method start()

ALWAYS WRITE @Override on the implementing method. It is not required by
the compiler here, but it catches the classic typo -- misspell the name and
you have silently added a NEW method while leaving the abstract one
unimplemented. @Override turns that into an instant error.


===============================================================================
PART 5 : THE ILLEGAL COMBINATIONS
===============================================================================

Four combinations are compile errors. Learn the REASON, not the list --
every one of them is the same reason: abstract means "a subclass must
override this", and each keyword below makes overriding impossible.

    abstract + final        final means "cannot be overridden / extended".
                            abstract means "MUST be overridden".
                            A direct contradiction.

    abstract + private      private members are not inherited, so a subclass
                            cannot see the method, let alone override it.

    abstract + static       static methods belong to the CLASS, not to an
                            object. They are not overridden -- they are
                            HIDDEN, resolved from the reference type at
                            compile time. (Polymorphism/Notes, mistake 2.)
                            There is no dynamic dispatch to make abstract
                            mean anything.

    abstract constructor    a constructor is never inherited and never
                            overridden, so abstract has no meaning on it.

Not an error, but worth knowing:

    abstract class + private constructor    legal, but then only nested
                                            classes can extend it.
    abstract class with main()              legal, and main() runs normally.
                                            main is static, so no object of
                                            the abstract class is needed.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : ABSTRACT CLASS WITH NO ABSTRACT METHODS
===============================================================================

Legal, and occasionally exactly right.

        abstract class Config {
            void load()  { ... }
            void save()  { ... }
        }

You are saying: "this class is complete and usable code, but it is a base
class -- it makes no sense on its own, so do not instantiate it directly."

You have already met this pattern in the JDK without noticing:

        java.util.Calendar        abstract, obtained via Calendar.getInstance()
        java.io.InputStream       abstract, has concrete read() methods
        java.lang.Number          abstract, parent of Integer, Double, Long
        java.util.AbstractList    abstract, the shared engine behind ArrayList

    Number is worth looking at properly. Every wrapper class you used in
    WrapperClasses/Main.java -- Integer, Double, Float, Long -- extends the
    abstract class Number. That is why this line compiles:

        Number n = 42;            // autoboxes to Integer, upcasts to Number

    and why  List<Number>  can hold an Integer and a Double at once.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : HOW IT CHANGES WHAT YOU ALREADY WROTE
===============================================================================

Go through your repo with this one question: "is the parent class a THING,
or a CONCEPT?" If it is a concept, it should be abstract.

    OOPS/Polymorphism/ShapesExample.java
        ShapesExample.area() prints "This is the shape class example".
        A shape with no area. Make the class abstract and delete that body.
        -> you will do this in ShapeArea.java

    OOPS/Inheritance/MultiLevelInheritance/LivingThingAnimalDog
        A LivingThing that is not any particular living thing cannot exist.

    OOPS/Inheritance/MultiLevelInheritance/ProductElectronicLaptop
        "Product" is a category. Nobody buys a Product.

    OOPS/Inheritance/HierarchicalInheritance/EmployeeSystem
        Employee is arguably real -- but calculateSalary() certainly is not
        definable at that level. Make the CLASS concrete or abstract as you
        judge, but that METHOD should be abstract.

    OOPS/Polymorphism/MethodOverriding/PaymentSystem.java
        Payment.pay() prints "Processing Payment of <amount>" -- a
        placeholder. But careful: WalletPayment calls super.pay(amount) and
        DEPENDS on that body existing. So here the right answer is to keep
        pay() concrete. Not every placeholder should become abstract.

    The deciding question for a METHOD is:
        "can I write a body here that every subclass would genuinely want?"
            yes -> concrete
            no  -> abstract


===============================================================================
PART 8 : TRAPS AND INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================================

  1. Can an abstract class have a constructor?
        Yes. It runs via super() when a subclass object is created, and it
        initialises the shared fields. See PART 3.

  2. Can an abstract class have zero abstract methods?
        Yes. It just means "do not instantiate me directly". See PART 6.

  3. Can an abstract method be static / final / private?
        No to all three. Each makes overriding impossible, and abstract
        demands overriding. See PART 5.

  4. Can an abstract class be final?
        No. Direct contradiction -- final blocks extension, abstract
        requires it.

  5. Can an abstract class extend a concrete class?
        Yes. Every class already extends Object, which is concrete.

  6. Can an abstract class have a main method and be run?
        Yes. main is static, so no instance is needed.

  7. If a subclass does not implement all abstract methods, what happens?
        Compile error, unless the subclass is itself declared abstract.

  8. Can you have a reference of an abstract type?
        Yes, and you will constantly:   Shape s = new Circle();
        You cannot create the OBJECT. The REFERENCE is fine. This is the
        single most common confusion in the topic.

  9. Are abstract classes useless now that interfaces have default methods?
        No. An interface still cannot have instance fields, a constructor,
        or non-public members. When you need shared STATE, you need an
        abstract class.

 10. What is the difference between an abstract class and a concrete class
     with methods that just throw an exception?
        The abstract version fails at COMPILE time; the other fails at
        RUNTIME, on a customer's machine. Prefer compile-time.

 11. Can an abstract class implement an interface without implementing its
     methods?
        Yes. They remain abstract and the first concrete subclass must
        supply them. See PART 2.


===============================================================================
PART 9 : PRACTICE LIST
===============================================================================

  1. Run AbstractClassDemo.java in this package. Then uncomment each of the
     lines marked ERROR, one at a time, and read what the compiler says.

  2. Do ShapeArea.java (PRACTICE 1) -- rewrite ShapesExample properly.

  3. Do GameCharacter.java (PRACTICE 2) -- abstract class with shared state
     and a constructor.

  4. Write an abstract class with a constructor that prints a line, plus a
     static block and an instance block. Create a subclass object and
     predict the print order BEFORE you run it. Check against PART 3.

  5. Prove PART 4 choice 2: make a three-level chain where the middle class
     implements only one of two abstract methods and must stay abstract.

  6. Try every illegal combination from PART 5 and collect the four error
     messages. Recognising them by sight is worth more than memorising the
     rules.

  7. Go back to LivingThingAnimalDog and make LivingThing abstract. Find
     out what breaks in main() and fix it.

  8. Open the JDK source of java.lang.Number (Ctrl+click Integer in
     IntelliJ, then click Number). Confirm it is abstract and see which
     methods are abstract and which are not.
